; =============================================================================
; Tunah tuner -- constant propagation
; =============================================================================
; Purpose       : Propagate a value to the places that use it. A copy is not an
;                observable result, so an e-class that holds a copy also holds
;                the copied value; once that equality is in the e-graph, every
;                use of the copy is a use of the value, and a use of a literal
;                operand reaches the constant the literal denotes. The
;                normalisation that puts a moved value into copy form is
;                stated once, in reduntant-instr-elim.tuner.
; Stage         : Instruction level machine IL, during value numbering.
; Vocabulary    : copy, sel and phi from the shared directory vocabulary. No
;                arithmetic operator appears here; purely algebraic identities
;                belong to alg-sim.tuner and literal evaluation to
;                const-folding.tuner, so a rule body appears in exactly one
;                file of this directory.
; Preconditions : A copy is an ordinary data movement with no observable
;                behaviour of its own, and it is not a barrier, so a use of a
;                copy is a use of the copied value on every path. Every rule
;                here replaces one spelling of a value with another and none of
;                them deletes a memory effect, so none of them can move an
;                observable operation.
; Cost model    : Both spellings of a value are equivalent under the size cost
;                model, so propagation never biases extraction; it only
;                enlarges the set of terms available to later rules.
; Termination   : cp-copy-collapse is a union of two e-classes and cannot add
;                a node. The other rules only delete copy wrappers, so each
;                application removes exactly one node from the matched subterm
;                and no rule can re-create it without consuming another node.
;                The set is therefore monotone and each site is rewritten at
;                most once per copy eliminated.
;
; Operator signatures required by this file:
; signatures: copy 1, sel 3, phi 2
; =============================================================================

; A copy denotes the value it copies, so the two belong to one e-class. This
; is the rule that makes every other propagation in this file, and every
; constant fold over a moved literal, reachable.
(rule cp-copy-collapse (copy ?x) ?x)
; A select of two copies is a select of the copied values, so the wrapper can
; be dropped from the first arm without changing the selected value.
(rule cp-sel-lift-copy-left (sel (copy ?a) (copy ?b) ?c) (sel ?a ?b ?c))
; Same, for the second arm; the condition operand is never a data operand.
(rule cp-sel-lift-copy-right (sel ?a (copy ?b) ?c) (sel ?a ?b ?c))
; A merge whose first input is a copy is a merge of the copied value, which is
; what makes the value available on the incoming edge.
(rule cp-phi-lift-copy-left (phi (copy ?a) ?b) (phi ?a ?b))
; Same, for the second input of the merge.
(rule cp-phi-lift-copy-right (phi ?a (copy ?b)) (phi ?a ?b))
; A select with a known true condition is its first operand, so no propagation
; of the condition is needed to know which value survives.
(rule cp-sel-const-true (sel ?a ?b 1) ?a)
; A select with a known false condition is its second operand.
(rule cp-sel-const-false (sel ?a ?b 0) ?b)
